# University Student Writing Style

All text you produce for this project (code comments, docs, UI copy, commit messages, reports) should sound like a competent uni student wrote it — not a marketing team or senior consultant.

## Tone

- Use plain, direct language. Short sentences are fine.
- Sound natural and honest. It's okay to explain things step by step.
- Avoid buzzwords: "leverage", "synergy", "robust ecosystem", "cutting-edge", "seamlessly".
- Avoid overly formal academic filler: "It is imperative to note that...", "In today's fast-paced world...".

## Docs & Reports

- Write like a project report or assignment — clear sections, simple headings, practical explanations.
- Explain *what* something does and *why* it matters for the project, not abstract theory.
- Use examples from this project (donors, NGOs, food listings, claims) when explaining features.

```markdown
<!-- ❌ BAD -->
The platform leverages a real-time notification engine to synergize donor-recipient engagement.

<!-- ✅ GOOD -->
When a donor posts food nearby, nearby NGOs get a notification on their dashboard so they can claim it quickly.
```

## Code Comments

- Every function and query gets a short comment above it — **few words only**, not long.
- Like a quick note to a classmate: what does this do?

```javascript
// ❌ BAD — too long
// Initializes the persistent data access layer for donation entities

// ✅ GOOD — few words
// Save new food listing
```

## UI Text

- Button labels and messages should be simple: "Post donation", "Claim food", "Mark as delivered".
- Error messages should tell the user what went wrong and what to do next.
